he actual forced current is $i_{\\text{force}} = 24\\cos{1000t} - 12\\sin{1000t}\\ \\text{mA}$.", "expert_answer": "None of the listed options is exactly correct.\n\nCorrect current: **\\(i_{\\text{force}} = 24\\cos(1000t) - 12\\sin(1000t)\\ \\text{mA}\\)**.", "expert_latency_s": 10.84, "stall_ms": 25, "relay_ms": 1247}
{"id": "q0268", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 20.16, "n_chunks": 21, "scores": [0.4509, 0.372, 0.4018, 0.3196, 0.3177, 0.3194, 0.4535, 0.4397, 0.3292, 0.2845, 0.3465, 0.3535, 0.4335, 0.3316, 0.3471, 0.387, 0.3672, 0.4057, 0.2962, 0.3049, 0.3755], "eot_score": 0.777, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "F. 20%", "query": "The percentage of children in Vietnam (age 8) who reported physical punishment by teachers in the past week in 2009 was about what?\n\nA. 60%\nB. 30%\nC. 10%\nD. 40%\nE. 70%\nF. 20%\nG. 90%\nH. 50%\nI. 80%\nJ. 100%\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "The percentage of children in Vietnam aged eight who reported physical punishment by teachers in the past week in two thousand nine was about what? (A) sixty percent, (B) thirty percent, (C) ten percent, (D) forty percent, (E) seventy percent, (F) twenty percent, (G) ninety percent, (H) fifty percent, (I) eighty percent, (J) one hundred percent.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The answer is F, twenty percent.", "expert_answer": "(F) twenty percent", "expert_latency_s": 5.03, "stall_ms": 24, "relay_ms": 229}
{"id": "q0273", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 23.07, "n_chunks": 24, "scores": [0.4122, 0.4131, 0.2999, 0.2919, 0.2627, 0.1922, 0.3444, 0.3955, 0.4601, 0.3811, 0.2539, 0.2118, 0.3248, 0.352, 0.3142, 0.4454, 0.3119, 0.4011, 0.43, 0.3692, 0.3031, 0.3384, 0.3927, 0.3344], "eot_score": 0.5291, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "D. x = 3 and x = 2", "query": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for x in the equation x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0.\n\nA. x = -1 and x = 6\nB. x = 0 and x = 6\nC. x = 1 and x = 2\nD. x = 3 and x = 2\nE. x = 1 and x = 6\nF. x = 3 and x = 4\nG. x = 2.5 and x = -2.4\nH. x = -2 and x = -3\nI. x = 4 and x = 5\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for x in the equation x squared minus five x plus six equals zero. a) x equals minus one and x equals six, b) x equals zero and x equals six, c) x equals one and x equals three and x equals two, d) x equals one and x equals six, f) x equals three and x equals four, g) x equals two pi and x equals minus two pi, h) x equals minus two and x equals three, i) x equals four and x equals five. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "To solve the quadratic equation $x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0$, we can use the quadratic formula $x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, where $a=1$, $b=-5$, and $c=6$. Plugging in these values gives $x = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 - 24}}{2} = \\frac{5 \\pm 1}{2}$. So the solutions are $x = 3 \\text{ and } x = 2$. The correct option is F. Answer F $x=3 \\text{ and } x=2$.", "answer_ms": 3449}
{"id": "q0288", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 115.11, "n_chunks": 116, "scores": [0.3946, 0.3421, 0.2498, 0.284, 0.3105, 0.2744, 0.3444, 0.4376, 0.2448, 0.3566, 0.4925, 0.4614, 0.5474, 0.2403, 0.2637, 0.4694, 0.5023, 0.3574, 0.3457, 0.2953, 0.3042, 0.439, 0.3923, 0.4986, 0.4764, 0.4201, 0.6194, 0.504, 0.4586, 0.3756, 0.3476, 0.3816, 0.3389, 0.3169, 0.4536, 0.2686, 0.5149, 0.3479, 0.4871, 0.3679, 0.5641, 0.4426, 0.3296, 0.5664, 0.3203, 0.3798, 0.3212, 0.4584, 0.3765, 0.5276, 0.4249, 0.2586, 0.2911, 0.2085, 0.2617, 0.3382, 0.524, 0.5328, 0.3031, 0.3889, 0.3099, 0.3284, 0.4446, 0.6239, 0.4348, 0.4462, 0.6216, 0.4015, 0.4052, 0.3433, 0.4634, 0.5255, 0.4013, 0.2681, 0.3482, 0.3975, 0.2235, 0.428, 0.543, 0.526, 0.3533, 0.2868, 0.5614, 0.3939, 0.4359, 0.3397, 0.5759, 0.3388, 0.5455, 0.4559, 0.5121, 0.2981, 0.4069, 0.366, 0.3404, 0.3454, 0.4404, 0.3262, 0.2931, 0.353, 0.3715, 0.3117, 0.3903, 0.3826, 0.442, 0.3745, 0.4181, 0.3787, 0.3774, 0.4612, 0.2252, 0.2758, 0.1814, 0.3208, 0.432, 0.3685], "eot_score": 0.7306, "eot_read_ms": 18, "reference_answer": "G. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car.", "query": "A highway patrol officer stopped a driver for speeding and found that the person had a suspended license. He allowed her husband to drive the car home but arrested her for driving under suspension. After securing her inside the squad car, he conducted a search of her vehicle. The officer found a bag of cocaine inside a zippered jacket on the back seat. He arrested her for possession and possession with intent to deliver a controlled substance. Prior to trial, the driver moved for suppression of the cocaine. Is it likely that the trial court will grant the suppression motion?\n\nA. Yes, the motion will be granted because a search of a vehicle requires a search warrant unless there are exceptional exigent circumstances.\nB. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have the driver's consent to search the car.\nC. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had the right to search the car for officer safety reasons.\nD. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had a reasonable suspicion to search the car due to the driver's suspended license.\nE. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had probable cause to search the car after finding out about the suspended license.\nF. No, the motion will be denied because this was a valid search incident to an arrest.\nG. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car.\nH. Yes, the motion will be granted because the cocaine was found in a personal item, not in plain sight.\nI. No, the motion will be denied because there is no expectation of privacy toward property inside one's car.\nJ. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have a warrant to search the jacket on the back seat.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A highway patrol officer stopped a driver for speeding and found that the person had a suspended license. He allowed her husband to drive the car home but arrested her for driving under suspension. After securing her inside the squad car, he conducted a search of her vehicle. The officer found a bag of cocaine inside a zippered jacket on the back seat. He arrested her for possession and possession with intent to deliver a controlled substance. Prior to trial, the driver moved for suppression of the cocaine. Is it likely that the trial court will grant the suppression motion? A. Yes, the motion will be granted because a search of a vehicle requires a search warrant unless there are exceptional exigent circumstances. B. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have the driver's consent to search the car. C. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had the right to search the car for officer safety reasons. D. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had a reasonable suspicion to search the car due to the driver's suspended license. E. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had probable cause to search the car after finding out about the suspended license. F. No, the motion will be denied because this was a valid search incident to an arrest. G. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car. H. Yes, the motion will be granted because the cocaine was found in a personal item not in plain sight. I. No, the motion will be denied because there is no expectation of privacy toward property inside one's car. J. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have a warrant to search the jacket on the back seat.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The suppression motion is likely to be granted. Under *Arizona v. Gant*, the search of the vehicle incident to arrest only applies if the arrestee is unsecured and within reaching distance, or if evidence related to the offense of arrest is likely to be in the car. Since the driver was secured in the squad car and driving with a suspended license wouldn't typically produce evidence of that offense inside the vehicle, the trial court will probably suppress the cocaine found during the search.", "expert_answer": "G. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car.\n\nUnder *Arizona v. Gant*, police may search a vehicle incident to arrest only if the arrestee is unsecured and within reaching distance of the vehicle, or if it is reasonable to believe the vehicle contains evidence of the offense of arrest. Here, she was secured in the squad car, and driving with a suspended license would not likely produce evidence in the vehicle.", "expert_latency_s": 3.57, "stall_ms": 20, "relay_ms": 2130}
{"id": "q0307", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 7.11, "n_chunks": 8, "scores": [0.4416, 0.4657, 0.529, 0.3744, 0.3377, 0.3848, 0.5058, 0.5303], "eot_score": 0.6886, "eot_read_ms": 19, "reference_answer": "Long names", "query": "The 1968 children's story Tikki Tikki Tembo by Arlene Mosel is a humorous take on the perils of what?", "transcript": "The 1968 children's story, Tiki Tiki Tembo, by Arlene Mosel, is a humorous take on the perils of what?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The 1968 children's story, Ticky-Ticky-Tembo by Arlene Mosel is a humorous take on the perils of having an excessively long name.", "expert_answer": "The perils of having an excessively long name.", "expert_latency_s": 2.21, "stall_ms": 25, "relay_ms": 831}
